.TH PRT-SNAPSHOT 1 "August 2026" "prt-snapshot 0.5" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
prt-snapshot \- manage snapshots of the package set installed by prt-get

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prt-snapshot
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prt-snapshot
stores the package names reported by
.B prt-get listinst
and can later inspect a stored snapshot, compare the current installed package
set with it, or restore package membership to that snapshot.

.PP
Snapshots are package-set snapshots, not full system snapshots. Package
versions, configuration changes, filesystem state outside package ownership,
runtime data, and the historical state of the ports tree are not captured.

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B clean
Remove all numeric snapshots managed by
.BR prt-snapshot .

.TP
.B store
.I msg
Store the current installed package set with the supplied message.

.TP
.B restore
.I num
.RI [ --dry-run | --yes ]
Restore package membership to snapshot
.IR num .
Packages not present in the target snapshot are removed. Missing packages are
installed with
.BR "prt-get install" .
Before execution, the complete plan is validated and missing packages are
ordered using
.BR "prt-get quickdep" .

.TP
.B diff
.I num
Show package-set differences between the current system and snapshot
.IR num .

.TP
.B show
.I num
.RI [ --packages | --json ]
Show snapshot metadata and package membership.
With
.BR --packages ,
print only validated package names, one per line.
With
.BR --json ,
print a machine-readable schema 1 JSON object.

.TP
.B list
.RI [ --json ]
List stored snapshots in numeric order.
With
.BR --json ,
emit a schema 1 JSON object containing a
.B snapshots
array with snapshot identifiers, creation timestamps, and messages.

.TP
.B help
Show usage information. This command does not require an initialized snapshot
store.

.TP
.B version
Show version information. This command does not require an initialized snapshot
store.

.SH RESTORE SEMANTICS
Restore intentionally uses
.B prt-get install
rather than
.BR "prt-get depinst" .

.PP
Before the first package mutation,
.B prt-snapshot
calculates and validates the complete restore plan. Missing packages are ordered
with
.B prt-get quickdep
but dependency information is filtered strictly to package names already present
in the target snapshot.

.PP
Dependency discovery is therefore used only for ordering. A dependency reported
by the current ports tree is never added to the restore unless it already
belongs to the target snapshot.

.PP
.B --dry-run
prints the exact removal and dependency-aware installation plan without changing
package state or pruning snapshot history.

.PP
Without
.B --dry-run
or
.B --yes ,
interactive restore prints the plan and asks for confirmation. Non-interactive
restore requires explicit use of
.B --yes
or
.BR --dry-run .

.PP
If plan validation or dependency-order resolution fails, restore aborts before
any package mutation. If package removal or installation later fails,
.B prt-snapshot
aborts immediately and preserves snapshots newer than the requested target.
Newer history is pruned only after a completely successful applied restore.

.SH SNAPSHOT INSPECTION
.B show
validates snapshot package data before producing output.

.PP
The default output contains the snapshot identifier, creation timestamp, stored
message, package count, and package list.

.PP
.B show --packages
provides a scripting-friendly package-name-only output.

.PP
.B list --json
outputs a schema 1 JSON object containing snapshot metadata in numeric snapshot
order. An empty store is represented by an empty
.B snapshots
array.

.PP
.B show --json
outputs a schema 1 JSON object containing the snapshot identifier, creation
timestamp, stored message, and package list. On success, stdout contains only
the JSON document. Diagnostics are written to stderr, and failures do not emit
partial JSON to stdout.

.SH SAFETY
Operational commands require root.

.PP
The snapshot directory
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots
must be owned by root, must not be a symbolic link, and must not be writable by
group or others.

.PP
The safety baseline introduced in version 0.4 validates snapshot identifiers and
package names, creates snapshots through temporary files, prevents numbered
snapshot collisions, serializes operations with a lock, limits
.B clean
to the numeric snapshot namespace, and preserves newer snapshots when restore
fails.

.PP
Version 0.5 additionally validates the complete restore plan before mutation,
adds explicit restore preview and confirmation, orders target packages using
filtered
.B quickdep
results, and prevents dependency discovery from expanding snapshot membership.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots
Persistent snapshot store.

.TP
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots/.lock
Temporary operation lock.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on successful completion and non-zero for invalid CLI usage or when
validation, snapshot capture, comparison, locking, dependency-order resolution,
package installation, package removal, or another required operation fails.

